@node	5.2, 5.3, 5.1, Chapter 5
@section A Register-Machine Simulator

In order to gain a good understanding of the design of register machines, we
must test the machines we design to see if they perform as expected.  One way
to test a design is to hand-simulate the operation of the controller, as in
@ref{Exercise 5.5}.  But this is extremely tedious for all but the simplest
machines.  In this section we construct a simulator for machines described in
the register-machine language.  The simulator is a Kotlin library with four
interface entry points.  The first uses a description of a register machine to
construct a model of the machine (a data structure whose parts correspond to
the parts of the machine to be simulated), and the other three allow us to
simulate the machine by manipulating the model:

@example
makeMachine(@var{register-names}, @var{operations}, @var{controller})
@end example

@noindent
constructs and returns a model of the machine with the given registers,
operations, and controller.

@example
machine.setRegisterContents(@var{register-name}, @var{value})
@end example

@noindent
stores a value in a simulated register in the given machine.

@example
machine.getRegisterContents(@var{register-name})
@end example

@noindent
returns the contents of a simulated register in the given machine.

@example
machine.start()
@end example

@noindent
simulates the execution of the given machine, starting from the beginning of
the controller sequence and stopping when it reaches the end of the sequence.

@noindent
As an example of how these procedures are used, we can build a model of the
@abbr{GCD} machine of @ref{5.1.1} as follows.  The controller is data, a
Kotlin list in the register-machine language of @ref{5.1.5}, with one
constructor call per line of the machine's diagram:

@example kotlin
public val gcdController: List<Stmt> =
    listOf(
        Label("test-b"),
        Test(opCond("=", reg("b"), constV(0))),
        Branch("gcd-done"),
        Assign("t", opSrc("rem", reg("a"), reg("b"))),
        Assign("a", reg("b")),
        Assign("b", reg("t")),
        Goto(GotoTarget.Lbl("test-b")),
        Label("gcd-done"),
    )
@end example

@example kotlin
val gcdMachine = either {
    makeMachine(listOf("a", "b", "t"), arithOperations, gcdController)
}
// Ok(machine) -- the assembler checked every register, label, and
// operation before the machine exists
@end example

@noindent
The first argument to @code{makeMachine} is a list of register names.  The
next argument is the operations table, a map pairing each operation name with
the Kotlin function that implements the operation (that is, produces the same
output value given the same input values); @code{arithOperations} holds the
section's arithmetic, including @code{rem} and @code{=}.  The last argument
specifies the controller as a list of labels and machine instructions, as in
@ref{5.1}.  Every assembly fault (an unknown register, an undefined or
duplicated label, an unknown operation) is a typed @code{MachineError} raised
through the @code{Raise} scope, so the @code{Ok} above means the machine was
worth constructing.

To compute @abbr{GCD}s with this machine, we set the input registers, start
the machine, and examine the result when the simulation terminates:

@example kotlin
either {
    val machine = makeMachine(listOf("a", "b", "t"), arithOperations, gcdController)
    machine.setRegisterContents("a", VInt(206))
    machine.setRegisterContents("b", VInt(40))
    machine.start()
    machine.getRegisterContents("a")
}
// Ok(VInt(2))
@end example

@noindent
This computation will run much more slowly than a @code{gcd} procedure written
in Kotlin, because we will simulate low-level machine instructions, such as
@code{assign}, by much more complex operations.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.7}Exercise 5.7:} Use the simulator to test the
machines you designed in @ref{Exercise 5.4}.
@end quotation

