@subsection The Machine Model

The machine model generated by @code{makeMachine} is represented in this
edition as an object of class @code{Machine}.  Where the book's model is a
procedure with local state that dispatches on messages, the techniques
developed in @ref{Chapter 3}, the edition's machine is an object whose local
state is the class's fields and whose interface is its methods; the object
boundary keeps the same separation, and the one method a variant machine
overrides, @code{execute}, is the seam the monitoring exercises of
@ref{5.2.4} use.  To build this model, @code{makeMachine} begins by
constructing the parts of the machine model that are common to all register
machines.  This basic machine model is essentially a container for some
registers and a stack, together with an execution mechanism that processes
the controller instructions one by one.

@code{MakeMachine} then extends this basic model to include the registers,
operations, and controller of the particular machine being defined.  First it
allocates a register in the new machine for each of the supplied register
names and installs the designated operations in the machine.  Then it uses an
@newterm{assembler} (described below in @ref{5.2.2}) to transform the
controller list into instructions for the new machine and installs these as
the machine's instruction sequence.  @code{MakeMachine} returns as its value
the modified machine model.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun makeMachine(
    registerNames: List<Reg>,
    operations: Map<String, Op>,
    controller: List<Stmt>,
): Machine = Machine(registerNames, operations).apply { install(controller) }
@end example

@subsubheading Registers

The book represents a register as a procedure with local state, as in
@ref{Chapter 3}; the edition's register is an object holding one machine
word.  The class @code{Register} creates a register that holds a value that
can be accessed or changed:

@example kotlin
public open class Register(
    public val name: Reg,
) {
    private var cell: Value = unassigned

    /** The register's contents. */
    public val content: Value get() = cell

    /** Writes [value] into the register. */
    public open fun store(value: Value) {
        cell = value
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
A register starts @code{unassigned}, the book's @code{*unassigned*} sentinel
held as a symbol value.  The name rides along because the register's own
reports need it (@ref{Exercise 5.18} traces every store by it).  The
following procedures are used to access registers: @code{register.content}
reads the cell, and @code{register.store(value)} is the single write path.
The write path is the whole reason @code{store} is a method and not a field
write: exercise 5.18's traced register overrides exactly this one method.

@subsubheading The stack

We can also represent a stack as an object with local state.  The class
@code{MachineStack} creates a stack whose local state consists of the items
on the stack.  A stack accepts requests to @code{push} an item onto the
stack, to @code{pop} the top item off the stack and return it, and to
@code{initialize} the stack to empty.

@example kotlin
public class MachineStack {
    private val entries = ArrayDeque<Value>()

    /** Push [value], advancing the monitoring counters of 5.2.4. */
    public fun push(value: Value) {
        entries.addLast(value)
    }

    /** Pops the top word; [reg] names the `restore` that asked, so an
     *  empty stack is the typed underflow naming the register. */
    public fun pop(
        reg: Reg,
        r: Raise<MachineError>,
    ): Value = entries.removeLastOrNull() ?: r.raise(MachineError.StackUnderflow(reg))

    /** The book's `initialize-stack`. */
    public fun initialize() {
        entries.clear()
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
The book's stack signals ``Empty stack: POP'' as a host error; this edition's
stack raises the typed @code{StackUnderflow} fault through the run's
@code{Raise} scope, naming the register whose @code{restore} walked past the
bottom.  (The counters that 5.2.4 hangs on the same class are already in
place here; they only observe @code{push}.)

@subsubheading The basic machine

The @code{Machine} class, shown in
@ref{Figure 5.13,,@w{Figure 5.13}}, constructs
an object whose local state consists of a stack, an initially empty
instruction sequence, a table of operations that initially contains an
operation to initialize the stack, and a @newterm{register table} that
initially contains two registers, named @code{flag} and @code{pc} (for
``program counter'').  The method @code{allocateRegister} adds new entries to
the register table, and the method @code{registerFor} looks up registers in
the table.

@noindent
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.13}Figure 5.13:} @math{\downarrow} The @code{Machine}
class, which implements the basic machine model.

@example kotlin
public open class Machine(
    registerNames: List<Reg>,
    userOperations: Map<String, Op>,
    private val deriveRegisters: Boolean = false,
) {
    /** The machine's registers by name, allocated in the given order. */
    public val registers: MutableMap<Reg, Register> = LinkedHashMap()

    /** The monitored stack. */
    public val stack: MachineStack = MachineStack()

    /** Everything the machine's operations print appends here. */
    public val transcript: StringBuilder = StringBuilder()

    /** The flag register: `test` writes it, `branch` reads it. */
    public val flag: Register = Register("flag")

    /** The operations table: the basic-machine operations first, then
     *  the caller's, which may override them. */
    public val operations: Map<String, Op> =
        buildMap {
            put("initialize-stack") { _ ->
                stack.initialize()
                VNil
            }
            putAll(userOperations)
        }

    /** The program counter: the index of the next instruction to execute. */
    public var pc: Int = 0

    /** The assembled instructions; execute() walks these. */
    protected var insts: List<Inst> = emptyList()

    /** True once pc has walked off the end: the stop address, where a
     *  trailing label points. */
    public val halted: Boolean get() = pc >= insts.size

    init {
        registerNames.forEach { allocateRegister(it) }
    }

    /** Allocates one register; a duplicated name is a host bug. */
    public fun allocateRegister(name: Reg): Register {
        check(name !in registers) { "multiply defined register: $name" }
        return newRegister(name).also { registers[name] = it }
    }

    /** Builds the register named [name]; 5.18's traced registers
     *  override this one factory. */
    protected open fun newRegister(name: Reg): Register = Register(name)

    /** The register an instruction names; an unallocated name is an
     *  assembly error, unless the machine derives its registers from
     *  the controller (exercise 5.13), in which case first sight
     *  allocates. */
    context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
    public fun registerFor(name: Reg): Register {
        registers[name]?.let { return it }
        if (deriveRegisters) {
            return allocateRegister(name)
        }
        return r.raise(MachineError.UnknownRegister(name))
    }

    /** Assembles [controller] and installs it as the instruction
     *  sequence. */
    context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
    public fun install(controller: List<Stmt>) {
        install(assemble(controller, this))
    }

    /** Installs an already-assembled program (the seam the variant
     *  assemblers of exercises 5.9 to 5.11 plug). */
    public fun install(program: CompiledProgram) {
        insts = program.insts
        labels = program.labels
    }

    /** Runs the machine from the beginning of the controller sequence
     *  and stops when it reaches the end. */
    context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
    public fun start() {
        pc = 0
        execute()
    }

    /** The execution loop: runs the instruction under pc and repeats
     *  until the sequence ends. Exercises 5.15 to 5.19 override this
     *  one method to count, trace, or stop between instructions. */
    context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
    protected open fun execute() {
        while (pc < insts.size) {
            insts[pc].exec(r)
        }
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
(The constructor's @code{deriveRegisters} flag stays false here; exercise
5.13 turns it on.)

The @code{flag} register is used to control branching in the simulated machine.
@code{Test} instructions set the contents of @code{flag} to the result of the
test (true or false).  @code{Branch} instructions decide whether or not to
branch by examining the contents of @code{flag}.

The @code{pc} determines the sequencing of instructions as the machine
runs.  This sequencing is implemented by the method @code{execute}.  Where
the book's @code{pc} is a register whose contents is the not-yet-executed
tail of the instruction sequence, the edition's @code{pc} is an integer
field: the index of the next instruction to execute.  In the simulation
model, each machine instruction is a data structure that includes a function
of no arguments, called the @newterm{instruction execution procedure}, such
that calling this function simulates executing the
instruction.  @code{Execute} gets that instruction, executes it by calling
the instruction execution procedure, and repeats this cycle until there are
no more
instructions to execute (i.e., until @code{pc} indexes past the end of the
instruction sequence, which is also what @code{halted} reports).

@noindent
As part of its operation, each instruction execution procedure modifies
@code{pc} to indicate the next instruction to be executed.  @code{Branch} and
@code{goto} instructions change @code{pc} to point to the new destination.  All
other instructions simply advance @code{pc}, making it point to the next
instruction in the sequence.  Observe that the loop in @code{execute} calls
one execution procedure after another, but this does not produce an infinite
loop confined to one instruction, because running the instruction execution
procedure changes the contents of @code{pc}.

Notice that starting the machine is accomplished by setting @code{pc} to the
beginning of the instruction sequence and calling @code{execute}.

For convenience, we provide an alternate procedural interface to a machine's
@code{start} operation, as well as procedures to set and examine register
contents, as specified at the beginning of @ref{5.2}:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun Machine.getRegisterContents(name: Reg): Value = registerFor(name).content

context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun Machine.setRegisterContents(
    name: Reg,
    value: Value,
) {
    registerFor(name).store(value)
}
@end example

@noindent
(@code{start} is the machine's own method.)  These procedures (and many
procedures in @ref{5.2.2} and @ref{5.2.3})
use the following to look up the register with a given name in a given machine:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun Machine.registerFor(name: Reg): Register
@end example

