@subsection The Assembler

The assembler transforms the sequence of controller expressions for a machine
into a corresponding list of machine instructions, each with its execution
procedure.  Overall, the assembler is much like the evaluators we studied in
@ref{Chapter 4}---there is an input language (in this case, the
register-machine language) and we must perform an appropriate action for each
type of expression in the language.

The technique of producing an execution procedure for each instruction is just
what we used in @ref{4.1.7} to speed up the evaluator by separating
analysis from runtime execution.  As we saw in @ref{Chapter 4}, much useful
analysis of Scheme expressions could be performed without knowing the actual
values of variables.  Here, analogously, much useful analysis of
register-machine-language expressions can be performed without knowing the
actual contents of machine registers.  For example, we can replace references
to registers by pointers to the register objects, and we can replace references
to labels by indices into the instruction sequence that the label
designates.

Before it can generate the instruction execution procedures, the assembler must
know what all the labels refer to, so it begins by scanning the controller text
to separate the labels from the instructions.  As it scans the text, it
constructs both a list of instructions and a table that associates each label
with an index into that list.  Then the assembler pairs each instruction with
the execution procedure for it.

The @code{assemble} procedure is the main entry to the assembler.  It takes the
controller text and the machine model as arguments and returns the assembled
program to be stored in the model.  @code{Assemble} calls
@code{extractLabels} to build the initial instruction list and label table
from the supplied controller text, then pairs every instruction with the
execution procedure @code{executionProcedure} generates for it -- the work of
the book's @code{update-insts!} -- and, walking the instructions once, also
gathers the summary of exercise 5.12:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun assemble(
    controller: List<Stmt>,
    machine: Machine,
    strictLabels: Boolean = false,
): CompiledProgram {
    val (insts, labels) = extractLabels(controller)
    val compiled =
        insts.map { inst -> Inst(renderStmt(inst), executionProcedure(inst, machine, labels, strictLabels)) }
    return CompiledProgram(compiled, labels, summarize(insts, labels))
}
@end example

@noindent
@code{ExtractLabels} takes the controller, the sequence of controller
instruction expressions, and answers two values: (1) a list @code{insts} of
instruction statements, each an instruction from the controller; and (2) a
table called @code{labels}, which associates each label from the controller
with the position in the list @code{insts} that the label
designates.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun extractLabels(controller: List<Stmt>): Pair<List<Stmt>, Map<String, Int>> {
    val insts = ArrayList<Stmt>()
    val labels = LinkedHashMap<String, Int>()
    for (stmt in controller) {
        when (stmt) {
            is Label -> {
                if (stmt.name in labels) r.raise(MachineError.DuplicateLabel(stmt.name)) // 5.8's refusal
                labels[stmt.name] = insts.size
            }
            else -> insts.add(stmt)
        }
    }
    return insts to labels
}
@end example

@noindent
@code{ExtractLabels} works by sequentially scanning the elements of the
controller and accumulating the @code{insts} and the @code{labels}.  If an
element is a label definition an appropriate entry is added to the
@code{labels} table, naming the index of the instruction that follows it; a
trailing label therefore names the stop address, one past the last
instruction.  Otherwise the element is accumulated onto the
@code{insts} list.@footnote{The book's scan passes a @code{receive} procedure
to itself, a way to get @code{extract-labels} to effectively return two
values---@code{labels} and @code{insts}---without explicitly making a
compound data structure to hold them.  A Kotlin function returns one value,
so this edition's scan is the pair-returning alternative the text considers:
@code{insts to labels}.  An argument like @code{receive} that is the next
procedure to be invoked is a ``continuation''; recall that continuations also
implement the backtracking control structure in the @code{amb} evaluator in
@ref{4.3.3}.}

@noindent
The book's @code{update-insts!} modifies the instruction list, which
initially contains only the text of the instructions, to include the
corresponding execution procedures.  The edition's instruction is built once,
already paired:

@example kotlin
public class Inst(
    public val text: String,
    public val exec: Exec,
)

public typealias Exec = (Raise<MachineError>) -> Unit
@end example

@noindent
The machine instruction data structure simply pairs the instruction text with
the corresponding execution procedure.  The execution procedure is produced
in the same pass that scans the instruction, so the book's second traversal
is unnecessary, but the pairing is the same.

The instruction text is not used by our simulator, but it is handy to keep
around for debugging (see @ref{Exercise 5.16}, which prints it, and
@ref{Exercise 5.17}, which hangs the label currently in effect beside it).

Elements of the label table are the map's entries, a label name paired with
the instruction index it designates:

@example kotlin
labels[labelName] = insts.size
@end example

@noindent
Entries will be looked up in the table with

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun lookupLabel(
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
    name: String,
): Int = labels[name] ?: r.raise(MachineError.UnknownLabel(name))
@end example

@noindent
the book's ``Undefined label: ASSEMBLE'' being the typed
@code{UnknownLabel} fault.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.8}Exercise 5.8:} The following register-machine code
is ambiguous, because the label @code{here} is defined more than once:

@example
start
  (goto (label here))
here
  (assign a (const 3))
  (goto (label there))
here
  (assign a (const 4))
  (goto (label there))
there
@end example

With the simulator as written, what will the contents of register @code{a} be
when control reaches @code{there}?  Modify the assembler's label scan
procedure
so that the assembler will signal an error if the same label name is used to
indicate two different locations.
@end quotation

