@subsection Generating Execution Procedures for Instructions

The assembler calls @code{executionProcedure} to generate the execution
procedure for an instruction.  Like the @code{analyze} procedure in the
evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}, this dispatches on the type of instruction to
generate the appropriate execution procedure.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun executionProcedure(
    stmt: Stmt,
    machine: Machine,
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
): Exec =
    when (stmt) {
        is Assign -> assignProc(stmt, machine, labels)
        is Test -> testProc(stmt, machine, labels)
        is Branch -> branchProc(stmt, machine, labels)
        is Goto -> gotoProc(stmt, machine, labels)
        is Save -> saveProc(stmt, machine)
        is Restore -> restoreProc(stmt, machine)
        is Perform -> performProc(stmt, machine, labels)
        is Label -> throw IllegalStateException("a label names a place, it does not execute")
    }
@end example

@noindent
(A label never reaches here; the scan removed it.  The dispatch's
@code{strictLabels} flag of the full assembler, exercise 5.9's strictness
seam, is dropped from this base listing and restored below.)  For each type
of instruction in the register-machine language, there is a
generator that builds an appropriate execution procedure.  The details of these
procedures determine both the syntax and meaning of the individual instructions
in the register-machine language.  We use data abstraction to isolate the
detailed syntax of register-machine expressions from the general execution
mechanism, as we did for evaluators in @ref{4.1.2}, by using the runtime's
statement and source types to extract and classify the parts of an
instruction.  Each generator runs once, at assembly time, inside the same
@code{Raise} scope the run will use; the execution procedure it returns
captures the machine's data paths (registers, flag, stack) and advances
@code{pc} itself.

@subsubheading @code{Assign} instructions

The @code{assignProc} generator handles @code{assign} instructions:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun assignProc(
    inst: Assign,
    machine: Machine,
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
): Exec {
    val target = machine.registerFor(inst.reg)
    val source = primitiveExp(inst.src, machine, labels)
    return { r ->
        target.store(source(r))
        machine.pc += 1
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
@code{AssignProc} takes the target register and the value expression straight
from the @code{Assign} statement's fields.  The book's selector procedures
(@code{assign-reg-name}, @code{assign-value-exp}) are not needed: the
runtime's statement types already carry their parts as fields, so a
malformed instruction is unrepresentable.

The register name is looked up with @code{registerFor} to produce the target
register object.  The value expression is passed to @code{operationValue}
if the value is the result of an operation (a @code{Source.OpSrc}), and to
@code{primitiveExp}
otherwise.  These procedures (shown below) parse the value expression and
produce a value procedure for the value.  This is a function of the raise
scope, called @code{value-proc} in the book, which will be evaluated during
the simulation to produce the actual value to be assigned to the register.
Notice
that the work of looking up the register name and parsing the value expression
is performed just once, at assembly time, not every time the instruction is
simulated.  This saving of work is the reason we use execution procedures, and
corresponds directly to the saving in work we obtained by separating program
analysis from execution in the evaluator of @ref{4.1.7}.

The result returned by @code{assignProc} is the execution procedure for the
@code{assign} instruction.  When this procedure is called (by the machine
model's @code{execute} loop), it sets the contents of the target register
to the result obtained by executing @code{source}.  Then it advances the
@code{pc} to the next instruction by incrementing the field,
@code{machine.pc += 1}.

@noindent
The increment is the normal termination for all instructions except
@code{branch} and @code{goto}.

@subsubheading @code{Test}, @code{branch}, and @code{goto} instructions

@code{TestProc} handles @code{test} instructions in a similar way.  It
takes the expression that specifies the condition to be tested and generates
a value procedure for it.  At simulation time, the procedure for the
condition is called, the result is assigned to the @code{flag} register, and
the @code{pc} is advanced:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun testProc(
    inst: Test,
    machine: Machine,
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
): Exec {
    val condition =
        when (val cond = inst.cond) {
            is OpCond -> operationValue(cond.name, cond.args, machine, labels)
        }
    return { r ->
        machine.flag.store(condition(r))
        machine.pc += 1
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
The book's generator faults on a @code{test} whose condition is not an
operation call; the runtime's @code{Test} carries an @code{OpCond} and
nothing else, so the malformed case is unrepresentable.

The execution procedure for a @code{branch} instruction checks the contents of
the @code{flag} register and either sets the contents of the @code{pc} to the
branch destination (if the branch is taken) or else just advances the @code{pc}
(if the branch is not taken).  Notice that the indicated destination in a
@code{branch} instruction must be a label; the runtime's @code{Branch}
carries only a label name, which enforces this.  Notice also that the label is
looked up at assembly time, not
each time the @code{branch} instruction is simulated.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun branchProc(
    inst: Branch,
    machine: Machine,
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
): Exec {
    val address = lookupLabel(labels, inst.label)
    return { r ->
        machine.pc =
            when (val flagValue = machine.flag.content) {
                is VBool -> if (flagValue.b) address else machine.pc + 1
                else -> r.raise(MachineError.BranchWithoutTest)
            }
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
The book reads the flag as Scheme truth; this edition's flag holds a
@code{VBool} after any @code{test}, and a @code{branch} that reads it before
any @code{test} set it is the typed @code{BranchWithoutTest} fault.

A @code{goto} instruction is similar to a branch, except that the destination
may be specified either as a label or as a register, and there is no condition
to check---the @code{pc} is always set to the new destination.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun gotoProc(
    inst: Goto,
    machine: Machine,
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
): Exec =
    when (val dest = inst.to) {
        is GotoTarget.Lbl -> {
            val address = lookupLabel(labels, dest.name)
            val exec: Exec = { _ -> machine.pc = address }
            exec
        }
        is GotoTarget.ByReg -> {
            val target = machine.registerFor(dest.reg)
            val exec: Exec =
                { r ->
                    machine.pc =
                        when (val value = target.content) {
                            is VInt -> value.n.toInt()
                            else -> r.raise(MachineError.BadGotoTarget(dest.reg, value))
                        }
                }
            exec
        }
    }
@end example

@noindent
A register destination must hold a label address, the integer index the
assembler gave the label; a register holding anything else is the typed
@code{BadGotoTarget} fault.

@subsubheading Other instructions

The stack instructions @code{save} and @code{restore} simply use the stack with
the designated register and advance the @code{pc}:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun saveProc(
    inst: Save,
    machine: Machine,
): Exec {
    val source = machine.registerFor(inst.reg)
    return { r ->
        machine.stack.push(source.content)
        machine.pc += 1
    }
}

context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun restoreProc(
    inst: Restore,
    machine: Machine,
): Exec {
    val target = machine.registerFor(inst.reg)
    return { r ->
        target.store(machine.stack.pop(inst.reg, r))
        machine.pc += 1
    }
}
@end example

@noindent
The final instruction type, handled by @code{performProc}, generates an
execution procedure for the action to be performed.  At simulation time, the
action procedure is executed and the @code{pc} advanced.

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun performProc(
    inst: Perform,
    machine: Machine,
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
): Exec {
    val action =
        when (val act = inst.act) {
            is OpAct -> operationValue(act.name, act.args, machine, labels)
        }
    return { r ->
        action(r)
        machine.pc += 1
    }
}
@end example

@subsubheading Execution procedures for subexpressions

The value of a @code{reg}, @code{label}, or @code{const} expression may be
needed for assignment to a register (@code{assignProc}) or for input to an
operation (@code{operationValue}, below).  The following procedure
generates value procedures to produce values for these expressions during
the simulation:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun primitiveExp(
    exp: Source,
    machine: Machine,
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
): ValueProc =
    when (exp) {
        is Source.RegSrc -> {
            val source = machine.registerFor(exp.reg)
            val read: ValueProc = { _ -> source.content }
            read
        }
        is Source.ConstSrc -> {
            val value = exp.v
            val read: ValueProc = { _ -> value }
            read
        }
        is Source.LabelSrc -> {
            val address = VInt(lookupLabel(labels, exp.name).toLong())
            val read: ValueProc = { _ -> address }
            read
        }
        is Source.OpSrc -> operationValue(exp.name, exp.args, machine, labels)
    }
@end example

@noindent
The syntax of @code{reg}, @code{label}, and @code{const} expressions is
determined by the runtime's @code{Source} type, a sealed hierarchy with one
case per form: @code{Source.RegSrc}, @code{Source.ConstSrc},
@code{Source.LabelSrc}, and the operation call @code{Source.OpSrc}.  The
book's predicate and selector pairs (@code{register-exp?} beside
@code{register-exp-reg}, and so on) become the type cases and their fields;
the dispatch above is exhaustive, so a malformed expression cannot be
constructed at all.

@code{Assign}, @code{perform}, and @code{test} instructions may include the
application of a machine operation (specified by an @code{op} expression) to
some operands (specified by @code{reg} and @code{const} expressions).  The
following procedure produces a value procedure for an ``operation
expression''---an operation name and the operand expressions from the
instruction:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun operationValue(
    name: String,
    args: List<Source>,
    machine: Machine,
    labels: Map<String, Int>,
): ValueProc {
    val prim = lookupPrim(machine, name)
    val argProcs = args.map { operandValue(it, machine, labels) }
    return { r -> prim(r, argProcs.map { it(r) }) }
}
@end example

@noindent
Observe that the treatment of operation expressions is very much like the
treatment of procedure applications by the @code{analyze-application} procedure
in the evaluator of @ref{4.1.7} in that we generate a value procedure for each
operand.  At simulation time, we call the operand procedures
and apply the Kotlin function that simulates the operation to the resulting
values.  The simulation function is found by looking up the operation name in
the operation table for the machine:

@example kotlin
context(r: Raise<MachineError>)
public fun lookupPrim(
    machine: Machine,
    name: String,
): Op = machine.operations[name] ?: r.raise(MachineError.UnknownOperation(name))
@end example

@noindent
the book's ``Unknown operation: ASSEMBLE'' being the typed
@code{UnknownOperation} fault.  The full assembler threads one more argument
through these generators, a @code{strictLabels} flag: with it set, an operand
written @code{(label x)} is refused at assembly time instead of handing the
operation the label's address (@ref{Exercise 5.9}).

@quotation
